ere is essentially a pendulum  swinging between extreme caloric  restriction and compensatory excessive  exercise to extreme binge eating all  under the guise of combating fear foods  for entertainment purposes now eric  himself has been quoted to say that he  doesn't consider what he does to be  healthy and that what he does would  probably be clinically classified as an  eating disorder like i said i'm not here  to diagnose eric so those are his words  not mine but if we were to look  objectively at the diagnostic criteria  for bulimia nervosa non-purge subtype in  the dsm-5 it would be characterized as  frequently eating large amounts of food  in a short period at least once a week  while simultaneously compensating with  excessive exercise to prevent weight  gain again i'm not making a diagnosis  but for strictly educational purposes i  do want to point out that it's not  abnormal for individuals with previous  histories of eating disorders to  essentially swap eds it's also a very  common disordered eating pattern to  essentially restrict heavily all day and  then consume all of your calories at the  end of the day almost as an act of  extreme delayed gratification i know  when i was struggling with my eating  disorder i actually did exactly that i  would basically eat the bare minimum to  survive all day while distracting myself  from eating with excessive exercise and  then by dinner i would just go from one  snack to the next until i fell asleep  going to bed often becomes the only way  to get oneself to halt the binge train  because once you open those floodgates  it feels like your starved body and mind  won't let you stop now whether or not  this is the case for eric i do not know  and i'm not suggesting that it is but my  concern is that people with previous ed  histories who watch his videos may get  the message that extreme mukbang style  eating challenges may be a good recovery  strategy because they see that eric  looks to be weight restored or he seems  to be at peace eating a big bowl of  lucky charms every night okay or  multiple bowls that is not true recovery  at least not for most people true  recovery would be nourishing your body  every day all day allowing yourself  lucky charms when you want them along  with other foods that make you feel good  not consistently eating so many bowls of  lucky charms that you feel sick and not  punishing yourself with restriction the  next day because you ate said extra  bowls of lucky charm i know it's so easy  to justify this behavior as food freedom  because that's what my eating disorder  voice told me too but there is a much  better way and it starts with quitting  the food challenges and other extreme  diet plans that require some form of  compensatory mechanisms or behaviors and  instead learning what actually feels  good to your body all day every day for  the rest of your life and on that note  that is all that i have for you guys  today a big reminder to please be kind  in the comments and another thank you to  buildbar for sponsoring this video if  you liked it be sure to give it the  thumbs up leave me a comment below on  who you'd like to see me review next  subscribe to this channel and i will see  you next time bye
 

